{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>I said in my heart.</b> I said in my heart on account of the children of men, whom Elohim has chosen above all creatures of the earth, and I saw that they are like beasts — they are to themselves in their thoughts. And the word על דברת is like בעבור על דברתי מלכי צדק (Genesis 14:18) — on account of your being a king of righteousness. And the lamed of לברם is like אמרי לי אחי הוא (2 Samuel 9:3), and לברם is on the pattern of ראם, from ברו לכם איש. And some say that לברם is a verb, the resh properly taking a dagesh, its root being לבררם, like לברר and ללבן. This is far-fetched, for in the pi'el construction with a dagesh the ayin is never found vowelless. And he says that they are a beast; its meaning is like a beast, and similarly ועיר פרא אדם יולד (Job 11:12). אש אוכלה הוא כי שמש ומגן — and if a grammarian asks how the lamed enters upon a past verb, with no parallel, the answer is that the bet and the lamed are brothers, and this combination is like that combination, and one rule for all. It is found in וגם הוא לבדו (Job 25:2), which is also unique."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {0 "The phrase אש אוכל' הוא כי שמש ומגן is textually obscure; translated hyper-literally. The citation בהכין לו דוד does not correspond to any obvious biblical verse and may be corrupt or a misattribution."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:27:29.766187615Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}